   "title": "WaterFlow README, Selecting the final waters"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:q1",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Question",
    "mira:Question"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "QUE - Can a flow-matching model predict ordered water positions more accurately than the current state of the art?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The previous best method, SuperWater, recovers only about 27% of waters at high precision. The paper asks whether a new generator and confidence model can do better, especially at sub-angstrom tolerance."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Introduction, paragraphs 3 and 6; Results: Model Overview",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.11.18.624208"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:q2",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Question",
    "mira:Question"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "QUE - How reproducible are deposited water molecules across otherwise identical crystal structures?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "If similar structures disagree on which waters are modeled, that variation limits any model trained and scored on deposited waters."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Crystallographic Water Placement Pareto Front, paragraphs 1-2"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:q3",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Question",
    "mira:Question"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "QUE - Is water prediction limited more by training data quantity or by data quality?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The authors compare a larger, lower-resolution training set with a smaller, higher-quality one, and test filtering individual waters by quality."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Training data size affects prediction accuracy"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:q4",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Question",
    "mira:Question"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "QUE - Does adding crystal lattice neighbors (symmetry mates) to the input improve water prediction?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Many surface waters are coordinated by atoms of neighboring molecules in the crystal, which a model that sees only the asymmetric unit cannot see."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Encoding Symmetry Mates Improves Prediction Accuracy, paragraph 1"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:q5",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Question",
    "mira:Question"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "QUE - Are predicted waters supported by the experimental X-ray data, not only by deposited models?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Deposited models are incomplete, so a predicted water scored as a false positive may be a real water that was never built."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Evaluating Water Prediction on Experimental Data, paragraphs 1-2",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.12922",
    "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2024.01.003"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:q6",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Question",
    "mira:Question"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "QUE - How does WaterFlow behave across inference settings, on predicted structures and at ligand binding sites?",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Users choose a confidence or density threshold, and many applications will use predicted rather than crystal structures as input."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: WaterFlow Inference on Predicted Structures and Protein-Ligand Interactions"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c1",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Water position prediction lags behind protein and protein-ligand structure prediction",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Protein structure prediction is near experimental accuracy for static structures, but water placement is still limited. Physics-based methods are slow and force-field dependent, and earlier deep learning methods remain weak at high precision."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Introduction, paragraphs 2-3",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.08.26.747373#quote-c1",
    "https://doi.org/10.1038/S41586-024-07487-W",
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.01.08.631967",
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.06.14.659707",
    "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.2c00306",
    "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.3c01559",
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.11.18.624208"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c17",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Simpler design choices may suit the data-limited water prediction task",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "WaterFlow uses flow matching instead of diffusion, a simpler atom-atom graph, ESM3 embeddings and GVP message passing. The authors speculate this passes information to water nodes more directly, but did not test each change."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Discussion, paragraph 2; Results: Encoder, Generator, and Confidence Model",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2106.03843",
    "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.ads0018",
    "https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2210.02747"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c3",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - WaterFlow outperforms SuperWater at every precision level, most at sub-angstrom tolerance",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Both models were trained and scored on the same cluster-split data. The gap in parity F1 widens when the match radius is tightened from 1.0 to 0.5 Å."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: WaterFlow More Accurately Predicts Water Molecules, Figure 1B-C",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.11.18.624208"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c4",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Random train/test splits overstate water prediction accuracy because homologs share water sites",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "SuperWater was originally trained and tested on random 8:1:1 splits. Retrained on sequence and structure cluster splits, its precision and recall were lower than first reported."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: WaterFlow More Accurately Predicts Water Molecules, last paragraph",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.11.18.624208"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c2",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Deposited ordered waters are a noisy target for training and evaluation",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Which waters are modeled varies with experimental conditions such as temperature and pressure, and with modeling choices, peak picking and phase bias. Real but unmodeled waters bound recall, and waters missing from the evaluated structure bound precision."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Introduction, paragraphs 4-5",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-2836%2868%2990205-2",
    "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0076-6879%2897%2977019-8",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S1600576716018719",
    "https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.05.30.729005",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S1399004715000292",
    "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.str.2014.04.016",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0907444911001314",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0907444912001308",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0907444910007493",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0108767386099622",
    "https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.14320",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0907444998012086"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c5",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Consensus waters across isomorphous structures set an accuracy ceiling well above current predictions",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Up to about 30% of deposited waters are not reproduced across otherwise identical structures. The best achievable F1 against consensus sites is 0.87 to 0.94, so there is still room above WaterFlow."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Crystallographic Water Placement Pareto Front, Figure 2B",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.26434/chemrxiv-2025-8526g-v2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c6",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Consensus waters are better supported by the experimental data than non-consensus waters",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Consensus waters have lower B-factor z-scores and higher EDIA. Structures that agree more with the consensus have better resolution and lower R-free."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Crystallographic Water Placement Pareto Front, Figure 2C-D, Tables 2-3",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.7b00391"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c18",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Training only on well-supported waters should give a more accurate model (tested hypothesis)",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Because up to 30% of the training signal is unreliable, the authors hypothesized that restricting training to better-resolved and better-supported data would help."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Training data size affects prediction accuracy, paragraphs 1 and 3"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c7",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Structural diversity of high-quality training data limits water prediction accuracy",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "At equal training size, the higher-quality SMALL set gives better F1. LARGE overtakes it only by adding clusters that SMALL lacks, and then also saturates."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Training data size affects prediction accuracy, Figure 3A; Discussion, paragraph 4"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c8",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Filtering individual training waters by EDIA and B-factor did not improve accuracy",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "The model trained without the per-water filter was slightly better at parity. The authors suggest the extra waters may regularize, but say this needs more study."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Training data size affects prediction accuracy, Figure 3B; Discussion, paragraph 4"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c20",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Models trained on static coordinates cannot represent partially occupied, fluctuating waters",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Many training waters are likely partially occupied and coupled to protein motion. Modeling protein, ligands and solvent as a joint ensemble could capture this."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Discussion, paragraphs 9-10",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S2052252524005098",
    "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.90606",
    "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.07574",
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.25.690589",
    "https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.01.19.699807"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c9",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Including symmetry mates in the input graph improves water prediction",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Symmetry mates restore the coordination environment of waters at crystal contacts. The gain is almost entirely for waters within hydrogen-bonding range of a lattice neighbor."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Encoding Symmetry Mates Improves Prediction Accuracy, Figure 4B-C; Discussion, paragraph 3",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S2052252524007784",
    "https://doi.org/10.1002/prot.20478"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c10",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Explicit crystal symmetry may benefit structure prediction more broadly",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Only one prior model the authors found includes symmetry mates or crystal contacts. Proteins, ligands and ions also form contacts across symmetry-related interfaces."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Discussion, paragraph 3",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1039/D3SC04185A"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c13",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - The confidence score tracks experimental density support of predicted waters",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Higher confidence thresholds give predicted waters with higher EDIAm and higher density. Above a threshold of about 0.7, predicted waters are as well supported as deposited ones."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Evaluating Water Prediction on Experimental Data, Figure 6A-B",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.7b00391"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c11",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Many novel predictions are real waters that deposited models omit",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Predicted waters with no deposited water within 1.0 Å often sit on positive mFo-DFc difference density, and always have more density support than nearby decoy positions."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Evaluating Water Prediction on Experimental Data, Figure 6B-E; Discussion, last paragraph"
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c12",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - WaterFlow-predicted waters fit the structure factors nearly as well as deposited waters",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Unrefined predicted waters give R-free only slightly above the deposited model, and light refinement narrows the gap further. The authors suggest WaterFlow could be used to model waters during refinement."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: Evaluating Water Prediction on Experimental Data, Figure 6F; Discussion, paragraph 10",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0907444912001308",
    "https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.14320"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c16",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - Density thresholding removes the resolution dependence of water count at a small accuracy cost",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "With a fixed confidence threshold, the number of predicted waters falls with resolution, as deposited counts do. Keeping a fixed number of top waters per residue avoids this, with slightly lower precision and recall."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: WaterFlow Inference on Predicted Structures, Figure 5A-B, Figure S5; README: Selecting the final waters",
   "references": [
    "https://github.com/prism-science/WaterFlow#quote-c16",
    "https://doi.org/10.1107/S0907444998012086"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c14",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - WaterFlow is less accurate on predicted structures than on crystal structures",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Predicted models carry a different distribution of coordinate error than refined crystal models, which can disturb hydrogen bonds. The confidence model likely needs recalibration for the input source."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: WaterFlow Inference on Predicted Structures, Table 4; Discussion, paragraph 7",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.ade2574",
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.06.30.662466"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c19",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - WaterFlow is untested on cryo-EM structures and nucleic acids",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "Training used only proteins from X-ray structures. Cryo-EM models have larger coordinate uncertainty, and waters around nucleic acids may fall outside the model's scope."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Discussion, paragraph 8",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-020-01051-w",
    "https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.01.23.634578"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "wf:c15",
   "@type": [
    "wf:schema-Claim",
    "mira:Claim"
   ],
   "created": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "modified": "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z",
   "title": "CLM - WaterFlow can recover critical binding-site waters, including ligand-bridging waters",
   "description": {
    "@type": "Item",
    "format": "text/plain",
    "content": "In a matched apo/ATP-bound pair, WaterFlow placed bridging waters and the water shifted by the adenine ring close to their deposited positions. The authors note further testing is needed."
   },
   "creator": "wf:creator",
   "bibliographicCitation": "Srivastava et al. 2026, doi:10.64898/2026.08.26.747373. Results: WaterFlow Inference on Predicted Structures and Protein-Ligand Interactions, Figure 5E; Discussion, paragraph 5",
   "references": [
    "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002464"
   ]
  },
